 brigham and  women's hospital and then of course my  advisor John Quackenbush so before I get  too far ahead of myself and outline for  the talk I'll discuss a little bit some  of the challenges from going from G  wasps to function genome-wide  Association studies and then talk a  little bit about calculating eqtls then  finally our attempts at modeling eqtls  as bipartite networks and then finally  trying to go from network properties to  biology which you know as someone who's  worked on gene regulatory networks  before and coming from a network physics  background I think that's probably the  most challenging aspect of some of these  problems which is what aspects of the  network topology are actually most  informative to the biology of the data  that you're working with and then  finally I'll discuss some future  directions that we have for some of this  eqtl analysis so earlier this year there  was a paper that came out in nature  talking about the body mass index and  the genetic contribution to that and so  this method or this paper rather used  three hundred and thirty-nine thousand  individuals and their genotypes and what  I think is particularly illuminating is  that the 97 low side that they found  accounted for only two point seven  percent of the BMI variation and the  genome-wide estimates suggest that the  common variation accounts for twenty  percent of BMI variations so you have  lots of these low side these these snips  that all appear to have weak effects and  there's another paper that came out in  October of last year looking at the  genetic contribution and genetic  variation in human height and you know  in this in this particular paper they  used a quarter million individuals  253,000 and they were able to find 690  seven variants that were able to explain  twenty percent of the phenotypic  variance but then to go to twenty one  percent they needed two thousand roughly  snips to get twenty four percent of the  variance yet they include 3,700 snips  and then to get up to twenty nine  percent of the phenotypic variance you  needed 9,500 snips so clearly the snips  that are associated with these these  phenotypes these complex traits there's  not just one that's driving the complex  trait many of these are not strongly  Mendelian but rather you have this large  group of snips that appear to be at  least associated with phenotypic  variance so you know in case you're not  already familiar with G wash genome-wide  Association studies aim to associate a  genetic variant usually a snip with a  trait and typically at a genome-wide  significance level meaning that the  p-value has to be less than 10 to the  minus 8 and so this is from a paper in  2013 but genome-wide Association studies  have identified more than 8,500  significant associations with more than  350 complex traits and you know that  these numbers are exploding as as more  data becomes available but the challenge  with these methods is that you go from a  single nucleotide polymorphism or single  variant to a cell or tissue organ level  trait and so how does how do you bridge  this this gap functionally because  eighty-eight percent of the snips and  the nhgri gos catalog are intronic or  energetic so they're not directly  affecting coding and in fact there's a  paper that came out from the gtex  consortium just last week that suggested  that number is more like ninety-five  percent of snips so the the issue then  it's you know where is the function when  you want to investigate this  biologically and so what people often do  is end in this gtx paper that came out  last week which i would highly recommend  reading  they took an expression approach so  instead of just looking at whether or  not the snip is associated with the  complex trait you can do an association  with a snip and then a jeans expression  level using gene expression and Geney  genotyping from a matched set of samples  and so typically what's done is looking  only at snips that are within a certain  window of the transcriptional start site  so a common choice is a plus or minus  one mega base around the transcriptional  start site and so then you take a  particular snip you look at the gene  expression for the gene and then you can  do some sort of usually a linear  regression between the allele frequency  of the locus and the expression level  and so one of the other reasons that  dumb that people focus on sis is that  these effects are often easier to  interpret biologically and they have  strong effects and so you're for example  you're looking here at a figure from  trends and genetics from Gilla at all in  2008 and the left panel here is showing  each blue dot is a snip in its position  from the transcriptional start site of  the hla-c gene and the y-axis is the  minus log of the p-value for the  association between the gene expression  for hla-c and the sniff presents the  snip dosage and so the red dot and the  left panel the one with the minus log p  value close to 11 there 12 you can see  the genotype versus the expression in  the right-hand panel for that and so you  can see that you know this is kind of a  canonical example of an eqtl where the  expression changes as a function of the  genotype in a linear fashion however  there have been a couple of papers in  the last year year and a half especially  looking at this concept of trans eqtls  which are those that are outside of this  window  the transcriptional start site and  there's some work suggesting that trans  eqtls and more salts type specific they  can be associated with many genes so you  can imagine you know one example of a  trans eqtl might be one where there's  the snip is located in the promoter  region of a gene that is a transcription  factor and it alters the expression of  that transcription factor and thus all  of the downstream targets of that  transcription factor are also affected  and so there was a nature genetics paper  that came out in October of last year  looking at these trans eqtls and two  very large cohorts where I think the  first one is five roughly 5,000  individuals of 5000 samples and the  replication cohort was on the order of  3000 and they found that these trans  eqtls many of them were replicated so  there there does seem to be to be  evidence that they exist and in addition  there was a paper that also came out  recently from Karl King's words group  showing that if you look at these trans  eqtls which may in the genomic sense be  far away they may actually physically be  very close and so if you this paper  looked at higher order chromatin domains  using hi-c data and found that there's  actually a very sort of rich structure  to eqtls when you include the proximity  based on these high see domains so  something that might seem far away  genomically is actually physically very  close so there's a I think there's some  good evidence to to suggest that trans  eqtls are biologically relevant and  interesting but one of the additional  problems with these is the computational  challenge so for example for some of the  data that will show later if you have  roughly a million and a half snips and  20 and gene expression for 23,000 genes  that's a huge number of Association  tests if you want to look at all  possible snip gene pairs and in fact um  you know this would take roughly  eight hours to run on one of the  clusters here at dana-farber so you know  it's it's very very intensive however  there was a paper that also came out  last year by Andres oblong and he  provided this package in are called  matrix eqtl which is a very nifty way to  do lots of eqtl analysis via large  matrix operations and as we use that for  this project and found that it's  actually very easy to use and I'd highly  recommend it and so this is a screencap  from his website and you can see there's  a little quote from a satisfied customer  from King's College London saying that  you know this just shorten my computing  time from a year to a couple of days and  so this really opens up the ease of  calculating many many eqtls but we think  that that this is a reasonable way to go  as well because what this is going to do  is then produce many snips connected to  many genes and I think this speaks to  our intuition about how genetic variants  affect complex disease which is that you  know with the exception of the highly  penetrant Mendelian diseases when we're  thinking about complex disease that  especially ones that are age-related or  are late onset you know these things are  the are being influenced by many snips  that are then you know potentially of  weak effect as in the case of the G  wasps associations you have thousands of  snips that are then influencing genes  regine regulation and cell function and  so as someone from a networks background  my intuition when I get data like this  is to then try to treat all of these  these eqtls as a network of links  between snips and jeans and specifically  this naturally forms a bipartite Network  which is just to say that your network  has two classes of nodes you have snips  and jeans and you only ever have links  that go between the two different  classes of nodes so you only ever have  links between snips and jeans  you don't have links between snips and  snips or genes and genes I will say that  you could consider extending this work  and drawing links between the snips if  you have epistatic interactions for  example or if you wanted to put gene  regulatory links between the genes if  one of your genes is a transcription  factor for example and you want to  include its targets but that's not work  that will consider here so briefly the  the data set that we use to create this  bipartite network we got it from the  lung genomics research consortium and  the the website for that you can see at  the bottom of the slide here and this is  a collection of lung tissue from of  samples using it with COPD and also  control COPD is chronic obstructive  pulmonary disease and I'll discuss the  disease on the next slide briefly here  but you can see that we have a mix we'll  need to when we do the Qt l's correct  for smoking as as most of the the lung  tissue came from individuals of a  smoking history and this gold stage you  see on the rightmost column is just a  clinical marker of disease severity and  it has to do with a marker of pulmonary  function and so you can see that we have  a broad spectrum of gold stages as  they're called and so COPD as I  mentioned is chronic obstructive  pulmonary disease and it is the third  leading cause of death in the United  States and it's common sub types which  are perhaps better known our emphysema  and chronic bronchitis and the disease  has a very strong environmental  component which is that cigarette smoke  is the leading cause of COPD however you  know from a genetics perspective the  interesting part is that uh you know one  estimate has it that only twenty-five  percent of smokers who did not  previously have COPD before smoking go  on to get it so not everyone who smokes  gets COPD so this is the data set that  we're using for the eqtl  calculation we used matrix eqtl we  controlled for age sex and Packers we  looked only at the genotype snips we did  not impute and we excluded snips that  had a minor allele frequency less than  five percent and then after that we use  matrix eqtl to consider all possible  snip gene pairs so I sort of outline  then of how we construct our network we  take a snip and gene and then we perform  the linear regression using matrix eqtl  and then if the result of that test  after doing all tests and doing an FDR  correction persists and trans separately  but both holding them to an FDR ten  percent if the snip and the gene have an  association that is then has an FDR less  than ten percent we consider it to be a  link in our network so we draw a line  between that snip and that gene and we  do that for all of our genes a knowledge  that all of our snips and then we wind  up with this network and so as sort of a  first test of what the network  properties looked like we investigated  the degree distribution and so because  this is a bipartite network we actually  have two degree distributions to  consider and if you're not familiar with  some of the sort of network science  parlance the degree is just the number  of links that a node has and so what  you're looking at here is just the  essentially the normalized histogram or  the frequency of nodes that have a given  degree so for the snip degree  distribution and these are both plotted  on on log log axes you can see that on  the left hand side here for the snip  degree distribution that the  overwhelming majority of the snips are  only connected to one gene so that's the  sort of upper left-hand corner of this  plot however you can see that there are  a few there are a few genes that have  a decent number of connections and so  you can I try to use the pointer here in  the sort of the right side of the plot  you can see there are a handful that are  connected to many genes and then we see  a similar sort of sort of distribution  for the genes and so I should mention  also that this entire network includes  about 30,000 snips and 3,400 genes so  what we what we wanted to do next is ask  whether or not this these distributions  were power-law distributed there's a lot  of literature suggesting that many  networks in the real world so  sociological networks technical networks  and biological networks have this  so-called power law degree distribution  and one of the reasons that people like  to talk about it is because these power  law degree distributions networks that  have those have these hubs and so the  idea is that if if a network has this  kind of distribution they're susceptible  to a targeted attack of one of the hubs  so you could imagine that if you removed  one of these nodes that's connected to  20 or 30 or 40 different genes that it  would significantly impact negatively  the connectivity of the network and so  the popular example in technological  networks is air transport for example  here in the United States if you were to  remove O'Hare Airport in Chicago you  would significantly impact the success  of air travel in the United States so  what we wanted to do is actually test  whether or not so they these  distributions were power law distributed  and oftentimes if you just look at them  if you were to plot for example this a  true power law distribution where the  probability that a node has a degree of  has degree X goes like X to the minus  alpha if you were to plot that on top of  this you could get a fit that looks  pretty good but because you're you're in  the log  space small distances can actually be  quite large so we used a method outlined  in a paper by Mark Newman Aaron closet  and cosmic shalizi which I highly  recommend as well which is called power  law distributions in empirical data and  they have a very principled way for  trying to determine whether or not your  distribution is power law distributed  and so the advantage there is that you  do the fit so you take your empirical  distribution and then you try to fit a  power law to it you estimate what the  exponent for the power law is and the  minimum degree at which your power law  activity begins and then you do a  randomization where you generate in this  case five thousand synthetic data sets  that have those parameters then you try  to fit a power law distribution on top  of that and ask how much better that fit  is and the advantage to this is that you  can get a p-value for rejecting the  power-law hypothesis so a p-value that  says you know how confident am i that  this distribution is not a powerful and  so it turns out actually despite looking  very power law distributed it is not the  snip distribution is not power law  distributed the genes so the p-value  here is is almost zero meaning that  we're very calm or fire it we're fairly  confident that we can reject the  power-law hypothesis it's a little bit  unclear for the gene distribution since  the p-value is hovering around point one  but regardless there do seem to be these  hubs in the network and so what we  wanted to know then was where the jiwa  snips in terms of the degree are these  the global hubs in the network are they  the ones that seem to be most connected  to all of these genes and it turns out  that that is not the case so what you're  looking at here is is the gene degree  distribution that I showed you before  but the red dots here are the is the  distribution for the jiwa snips that we  pulled in from the nhgri g wasps catalog  and so  what you can see is that for each of  these degrees the jiwa snips do seem to  be more highly connected for the low  degree so there are fewer jiwa snips  there's a lower fraction of jiwa snips  rather that have degree 1 compared to  the entire network however there's this  sort of desert in the right side of the  plot where all of our global network  hubs the eqt the snips associated with  many genes those are not jiwa snips so  we found this somewhat surprising  especially given the sort of global hub  hypothesis in in biology however when we  thought about it we think it makes a lot  of sense and it brought back remembrance  of a story from from World War 2 where  the British Air Force was trying to  figure out they had a limited amount of  armor that they could put on their  bombers and they are trying to figure  out the best place to do that and so  I'll give you a second and encourage you  to try to think if you're handed this  picture where you would want to put the  additional armor and so a statistician  in Britain at the time was handed this  but he was also handed this information  which were the bullet holes on all of  the planes that returned back from  bombing runs and so he looked at the  data and thought about it for a little  while and realized that