y to do is put nodes  together in a group such that the number  of links within a community or a group  is higher than expected by chance and  I'll go into that expected by chance bit  here in a minute but it's sort of a if I  explain it show you this picture it kind  of makes sense intuitively which is that  you have this network in the lower right  here and these gray blobs represent the  different communities and so you want to  make sure that you divide things up so  that there are lots of links within the  gray blobs and very few links going  between them and formally what these  community structure algorithms typically  do is they assign nodes to communities  such that the modularity queue is  maximized and so the modularity is just  a sum over all of the communities where  you take the fraction of network links  in community I and so  track that from the fraction of links  you would expect by chance and that  expected by chance uses the degree  distribution of the network that you  have one of the other advantages to  these community structure methods is  that unlike some clustering algorithms  you do not have to give it the number of  clusters a priori so that is one of the  things that it attempts to find and you  can imagine that that this might make  sense in a biological context you can  imagine that there might be a group  pathways that are have many links within  the pathway but they only have a few  coming in or going out and this is sort  of our vision of what these communities  might represent so one thing that we did  have to modify though from the  traditional community detection is that  we had a pipe we have a bipartite  network and so we always expect that  snips are never going to be connected to  other snips and that genes are all are  never going to be connected with other  genes and so our bipartite community  picture might look a little more like  something in the middle of the slide  here where you have groups of snips that  are the circles they may have lots of  links to the same set of genes but not  very many links to other sets of genes  and so we need a different null model  that accounts for that and I'll sort of  go briefly through this slide since it's  somewhat mathematical but I would refer  you to the paper by michael barber on  community detection of bipartite  networks where this came from and so  really the idea here the key is takeaway  is that this a IJ tilde here is just a  binary matrix where there's a one if  snip I is connected to GJ and 0  otherwise and so for genes that are in  the or four nodes that are in the same  community together you asked if the link  between the snip and the gene if that  exists how likely is that to have  happened given the numb  of links that the snip has and the  number of links that the gene ends and  so you can do this and then there are  some good heuristics so since it's  technically an NP hard problem to to  maximize this modularity so we went  ahead and implemented that for our  bipartite network and what we did then  is we found that we got a really  strongly modular neck network so the  modularity value that we got out was  0.77 which is you know the modularity  goes between 0 &amp; 1 we got 35 communities  and so a good sort of visual  representation of the the community  structure here is shown at the bottom  we're on the x axis you have snips on  the y axis you have genes and what we've  done is we've grouped all of the genes  together that are in the same community  and we've grouped all of the steps  together that are in the same community  as well and then each dot in this figure  represents a link the colored links are  those that connect jeans and snips that  are in the same community and then the  black links the sort of off diagonal  links if you will are ones that connect  jeans and snips that are in different  communities and so you can see that most  of the links in the network go between  genes and snips that are in the same  community together so you can sort of  see that this idea of edge enrichment  and so we had 35 communities out and  this is just another way of looking at  it I call it a seashell plot but what  we've done is we've grouped all of the  nodes that are in the same community  together and those represent those are  the circles here and so you can see  again that there's a much higher density  of links within these circles then there  are that go between the circles and so  this is all well and good but what we'd  like to do then is asked what kind of  biological enrichment do we see in these  communities do that do these communities  reflect anything  interesting and so we did a standard go  enrichment and of the 35 communities  that we found 13 are enriched for go  terms with a p-value less than 5 times  10 to the minus 4 and a couple of others  cut-offs for the overlap and whatnot and  so just to kind of give you a flavor of  some the enrichment we found one that  was associated with a microtubule  organization cell cycle on centrosome  and in addition we found this community  30 which it is enriched for immune  response stress response and t-cell  simulation which in the context of COPD  makes sense as of one of the large  features defining COPD is this chronic  inflammation and so there's a strong  immune and inflammatory response in the  lung tissue which is where we where we  got our genotyping n gene expression and  then finally we we have this community  18 which is chromatin assembly DNA  confirmation change and nuclear zone  assembly and this includes the hist 1 a  family as well so there does seem to be  some some very interesting biology here  which someone makes content makes sense  in the context of our our lung tissue  but the other thing that we want to do  and ultimately address is linking snips  to function and since we have roughly  30,000 snips what we wanted to do is not  just make a flat list of snips which is  you know we have these snips in this  community and then you know try to do  some sort of Fisher's exact test or  hyper geometric what we'd like to do is  be able to rank the snips in the  communities by some sort of topological  to Paula G that is relevant so what we  tried to do is what we did here is  scored the snips by their modularity  contribution so if you remember from a  couple of slides back the modularity is  really the sum of network enrichment of  Lincoln Richmond  uh over all of the communities so what  we can do is back up sort of back out  and say what is the modularity of  community H and that's given here and so  you're just subsetting the nodes that  are in community h and then furthermore  you can ask how much did note i  contribute to the modularity of  community h and so we define this course  color as we call it Q I h which is just  the fraction of modularity that snip I  contributes to community H of which it  is a part and so a sort of hand waving  or intuitive argument is that if you  look at this this community structure on  the left here with the red node and the  Blue Note you might say that the red  node is much more central to community  three because it has five links all of  which go to other nodes within community  three whereas the blue node which has  three links to other nodes in community  three also has a link going to community  too so our intuition would be to say  well clearly the red node is more  central to the community three structure  and that's really sort of the idea  behind our course for however we we  still need to figure out whether or not  these scores correlate or reflect any  sort of known biology so what we did is  we took a 259 disease-associated snips  from the NHGRI G West catalogs which of  all the nhgri gos catalogue snips and  then asked which of those are also eqtls  in our network and so we found 259 snips  that were also jiwa snips from the  catalog and then what we want to do is  ask what distribution of course cores do  our jiwa snips have and is that  significantly larger than for the non  jiwa snips so um we did this we tested  this in in two ways one we compute  KS test statistic where we asked whether  or not the distribution of jiwa score  scores is larger than the non-jewish  core scorers and then we did a  permutation test where we shuffled the  labels and we did this a hundred  thousand times and so the figure you're  looking at here is just the distribution  in blues the histogram of KS test scores  for the random shuffling and then the  the red dot here shows the true KS test  score for our jiwa snips versus the  others and then we also did a similar  wilcoxon test and so you can see for  both of them that the the p-value from  these tests the permutation p-value was  is 10 to the minus 5 and that's because  we ran 10 to the five tests and so in  addition to that we looked at the median  core score for our qo snips and asked  how much larger that is for the  non-jewish snips and it turns out that  the median course score for the gy snips  is 1.7 times higher than the median for  the non-jew escape so they do seem to be  more likely to be at the cores of these  communities the jiwa snips do uh in  addition we sort of looked at the  communities of which these jiwa snips  were apart and it turns out that 21 of  the 35 communities in our network had at  least one jiwa snip and we look then a  little bit at the function and we found  that this community 31 for example had  jiwa snips for COPD biomarkers smoking  behavior asthma we also looked at  community 30 which also had jiwa snips  for COPD mark biomarkers pulmonary  function interstitial lung disease which  is is also a sort of inflammation lung  disease and I would remind you that so  community 30 also had go term enrichment  for genes associated with T cell  stimulation inflammation and so on and  then community 18 here was also had  Jewish snips for psoriasis Crohn's  disease and cytokine response so we  think that this this approach to g wasps  which is to look at the expression and  the genotyping a due to eqtl analysis  and then ask you know instead of just  restricting our list so that we can make  sense of you the top 10 eqtls rather we  want to embrace the complexity of this  data and allow allow it to to reflect  the pattern of connectivity so a couple  of conclusions we think that by partek  networks are flexible way to handle  large numbers of eqtls and as the the  gtex program consortium continues to  grow and ramp up and as the availability  of whole genome sequencing and RNA seek  as those prices continue to fall which  john talked about a little bit the  falling prices of some of these assays  as those become readily available we  think the number of eqtls that are going  to be available to detect and and  compute it's going to grow and i think  this is a nice method to incorporate all  of those and to allow this sort of  agglomeration of weak signals that  points to biological function we also  found that community clustering can  highlight groups of biological elements  that are working together which we  showed from the the go enrichment and  also in the G wasps function and then  surprisingly we found that the Geo snips  our local but not global eqtl hubs and  that they often lie at the cores of  these communities and so some future  work we're thinking about integrating  epigenetic information for example  layering on encode data from the  relevant cell lines there's been some  work showing that looking at DNA  sensitivity can really inform some of  the eqtls in addition we're interested  in looking at the effects of methylation  on some of these eqtls particularly in  the context of co  e which is has a you know strong impact  from smoking which can affect  methylation and then we're also  interested in replicating these eqtls in  another lung data set and also comparing  the healthy networks with our disease  networks and I think that's another one  of the the advantages of computing these  core scorers is that it then allows for  distributions to be compared against  each other so you can ask you know which  are the most significant snips in the  disease network based on the core  scorers and then compare that with the  healthy eqtl network from lung and asked  whether or not those those snips  prioritized by the core scorers in the  disease case are also prioritized in  healthy case or it seems to be  differential between them and so with  that I'd like to thank my collaborators  peak Castaldi don de mayo Michael Cho  Craig Hirsch ed silverman all of whom  are at Brigham and Women's and the  Harvard Medical School and then some of  the other folks in the Quackenbush lab  marika cooler Kimberly glass and Albert  young and so thank you for your  attention I'd be happy to take any  questions oh and also my email is in the  bottom here J Platt at Jimmy harvard.edu  so thanks well thank you dr. platique  for that very informative presentation  before we start the Q&amp;A session I'd like  to remind our audience that you can  submit your questions in the Q&amp;A box  that will find by clicking on the green  Q&amp;A button in the lower left hand corner  of the presentation window we'll try to  answer all the questions we can and  we'll wait a minute to see if anybody  has any questions at this point in the  meantime I'd like to remind everybody  that this presentation will be available  for replay for six months after the days  of the slide presentation and we have a  question here this is from  and as did rich from the Institute /  systems biology do the eqtl hub snips  tend to have lower allele frequency from  the bullet hole analogy wouldn't want to  expect strong negative selection on  these snips so yes I think that's a  that's an excellent question I agree  that that I think you you would expect a  strong negative selection on the snips  that are significantly affecting gene  expression in a way that is that is  altering that is altering the function  of the cell so I think you know in the  context of these hubs we're still trying  to investigate you know what exactly  that is whether or not it's it's a  function of statistical association or  it maybe it's just some sort of kind of  neutral evolution that it's it's not  deleterious and so it's not swept away  it may not be functional even though its  associated so yeah thanks okay well once  again I'd like to remind everyone that  looked for an email from lab Rhodes  reminding you when this broadcast will  be available for replay and we invite  you to forward that announcement to your  colleagues who may have missed today's  event once again thank you for joining  us today and we'll see you next time you  buy
 

